  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Vienna Method",
    "overview": "The site is a sheet of pictorial statistics in the Isotype tradition. Results are counted out, not described: each figure is a row of identical flat pictograms where one symbol is one fixed unit, grouped in fives, labelled at the left, totalled at the right, and keyed underneath (\"Each clock is 10 minutes\"). A visitor can read the work at a glance and check the arithmetic if they care to.\n\nThe material is print: white ground, near-black ink, and a small set of flat Isotype colours that carry meaning (blue for the baseline, red for the result and the action). Structure comes from heavy ink rules and caps headings in one geometric sans (Jost, Futura lineage), not from boxes, cards, shadows or rounded corners. Density is low and scale contrast is high: very large caps headlines over small tracked caps labels, with long-form detail folded into drawers.\n\nHonesty is visual too. Indexed figures are labelled as indexed in the key line, half units are drawn as half symbols, and nothing is decorated that is not counting something.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "One symbol = one fixed unit; every chart has a key line naming the unit.",
      "Flat filled pictogram silhouettes with paper knock-outs, from one inline sprite.",
      "Blue = before/baseline, red = after/result/action; ochre and green are further categories only.",
      "One family (Jost, variable 100-900), caps for headings and labels.",
      "Ink rules (6/3/2/1px) as the only framing device; zero radius, zero shadow, zero gradient."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Blue Before, Red After Rule",
        "body": "In any comparison the baseline is blue and the result is red. Red is never used for a baseline, and blue is never used for a call to action.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Flat Ink Rule",
        "body": "Colours are solid fills. No gradients, tints-as-decoration, glows or transparency effects.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Family Rule",
        "body": "Jost is the only face. Hierarchy comes from size, weight and case, never from a second family.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Caps Name, Lowercase Explains Rule",
        "body": "Headings, labels and buttons are uppercase and tracked; sentences are sentence case.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Printed Sheet Rule",
        "body": "Nothing floats. Separation comes from rules and solid grounds, never from shadow, blur or lift.",
        "section": "elevation"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ruled, Not Boxed Rule",
        "body": "Content groups are opened and closed by horizontal rules. The only enclosed shape is the segmented toggle, a single 2px ink frame divided by 2px ink lines.",
        "section": "shapes"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do draw every number as identical pictograms where one symbol is one stated unit, and write the unit in a key line under the chart.",
      "Do colour the baseline blue and the result red, and keep red for the result and the hiring action.",
      "Do group pictograms in fives and draw fractional units as a clipped half symbol.",
      "Do label indexed or estimated figures as such in the key line.",
      "Do separate sections with the 6px ink rule and group content with the rule ladder (6/3/2/1px).",
